## Proof

Let $c$ denote the Banach space of all convergent (real or complex) sequences $x=(x_n)_{n\ge1}$ equipped with the supremum norm $\|x\|_\infty=\sup_{n\ge1}|x_n|$, and let $Y=c_0$ be the closed subspace of sequences converging to zero.

**Claim.** The quotient space $c/c_0$ is isometrically isomorphic to $\mathbb{R}$ (resp. $\mathbb{C}$ in the complex case).

---

### Construction of the isomorphism

Define the **limit functional**
$$L:c\to\mathbb{R}\ (\text{or }\mathbb{C}),\qquad L(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}x_n.$$

**Linearity.** $L$ is linear because limits are linear: $L(\alpha x+\beta z)=\alpha L(x)+\beta L(z)$.

**Boundedness.** For every $x\in c$,
$$|L(x)|=\left|\lim_{n\to\infty}x_n\right|=\lim_{n\to\infty}|x_n|\le\sup_{n\ge1}|x_n|=\|x\|_\infty,$$
so $L$ is bounded with $\|L\|\le 1$. Equality $\|L\|=1$ is attained by the constant sequence $x=(1,1,1,\dots)$, for which $L(x)=1=\|x\|_\infty$.

**Kernel.** $\ker L=\{x\in c:L(x)=0\}=\{x\in c:\lim x_n=0\}=c_0=Y$.

By the algebraic first isomorphism theorem, $L$ induces a linear bijection
$$\widetilde{L}:c/\ker L=c/c_0\;\longrightarrow\;\operatorname{range}(L)=\mathbb{R}\ (\text{or }\mathbb{C}),\qquad \widetilde{L}([x])=L(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}x_n,$$
where $[x]=x+c_0$ denotes the coset of $x$.

---

### Isometry

We show $\bigl\|[x]\bigr\|_{c/c_0}=\bigl|\widetilde{L}([x])\bigr|$ for every $x\in c$, where the quotient norm is
$$\bigl\|[x]\bigr\|_{c/c_0}=\inf_{y\in c_0}\|x+y\|_\infty.$$

**Inequality $\ge$.** For any $y\in c_0$, since $L(y)=0$,
$$\bigl|\widetilde{L}([x])\bigr|=|L(x)|=|L(x)+L(y)|=|L(x+y)|\le\|x+y\|_\infty.$$
Taking the infimum over all $y\in c_0$ gives $\bigl|\widetilde{L}([x])\bigr|\le\bigl\|[x]\bigr\|_{c/c_0}$.

**Inequality $\le$.** Set $a=L(x)=\lim_{n\to\infty}x_n$ and define $y_n=a-x_n$. Then $\lim_{n\to\infty}y_n=a-a=0$, so $y=(y_n)\in c_0$. Moreover $(x+y)_n=a$ for every $n$, hence
$$\|x+y\|_\infty=|a|=|L(x)|=\bigl|\widetilde{L}([x])\bigr|.$$
Therefore $\bigl\|[x]\bigr\|_{c/c_0}\le\bigl|\widetilde{L}([x])\bigr|$.

Combining both inequalities, $\bigl\|[x]\bigr\|_{c/c_0}=\bigl|\widetilde{L}([x])\bigr|$ for all $[x]\in c/c_0$.

---

### Conclusion

$\widetilde{L}:c/c_0\to\mathbb{R}$ (resp. $\mathbb{C}$) is a linear bijection that preserves the norm, i.e., an **isometric isomorphism**. Hence

$$\boxed{c/c_0\cong\mathbb{R}\ (\text{real case}),\qquad c/c_0\cong\mathbb{C}\ (\text{complex case}).}$$

